    95	
    96	After its fresh window, a replacement identity is newer than every initial identity; while it is uniquely the newest established identity, it receives at least $\min(s, \lceil r/2 \rceil)$ of the $r$ residual audits. In a fixed population in which honest providers do not replace, a provider that keeps replacing can stay the newest identity, so R3's targeting of replacements persists beyond the window; with honest churn it would share the newest positions with honest newcomers.
    97	
    98	\begin{theorem}[conditional renewal bound]\label{thm:renewal}
    99	Consider immediate-replacement plans whose play decomposes into independent, identically distributed cycles of an established spell followed by a recovery. Conditional on survival, each established round has expected profit $r_E$ and failure probability $q_E > 0$; the recovery has duration $D \ge L$ with $\mathbb{E}D < \infty$, profit $R_F$, and $N_R$ replacements, with integrable rewards and charges. The long-run profit per round is $\bar\Pi = (r_E/q_E + \mathbb{E}R_F - e\,\mathbb{E}N_R)/(1/q_E + \mathbb{E}D)$. If, in addition, truthful play earns a common rate $h$ in both states and, on every recovery history, every permitted fresh action earns a conditional expected surplus of at most $\epsilon \ge 0$ over $h$, then with $e = 0$
   100	\[ \bar\Pi - h \le \epsilon + \frac{(r_E - h - \epsilon)_+}{1 + q_E L}. \]
   101	\end{theorem}
   102	
   103	The factor $1/(1 + q_E L)$ is a bound for regenerative policies that satisfy the theorem's assumptions; we do not establish those assumptions for the implemented, finite-horizon R3 process, and the theorem covers neither the adaptive attacker families nor coalitions. No reported result depends on it.
   104	
   105	\begin{proposition}[terminal shading]\label{prop:terminal}
   106	In a round with zero continuation loss, a served provider starting from a positive truthful effort gains from small same-offer shading whenever $b > v_{\max}\,\phi(\tau/\sigma)/\sigma$.
   107	\end{proposition}
   108	
   109	At the first test's parameters the right-hand side is below $\const{0.067}$ and every provider has $b \ge \const{0.22}$, so no studied rule eliminates gain; all claims are bounded-gain claims. Proofs are in Appendix~\ref{app:proofs}; the theory checks of the first test are in the supplement.
   110	
   111	\section{The attacks we test against}\label{sec:attacks}
   112	Every unilateral attacker family reads only the provider's own history across its identities, the round, its own parameters, and the rule's public constants $T$ and $L$; it does not observe the remaining global budget or other identities' ranks. Coalition members may share their members' own histories. For each parametrised family a registered search (CMA-ES with restarts on training seeds) finds the attack. A rule's truthful reference is then selected on training seeds from one menu built from its D2+H3 and X7-SC searches and frozen; the W1 welfare searches, which need that reference, run after the freeze; coalitions use their own staged joint truthful reference; and no evaluation-seed episode of a market runs before all of its choices are frozen.
   113	
   114	\begin{table}[t]\centering\small
   115	\begin{tabular}{@{}lp{\const{0.46}\linewidth}lp{\const{0.22}\linewidth}@{}}\toprule
   116	Family & What it does and observes & Knobs & Where \\\midrule
   117	D2 & Adaptive claim markup that falls after a failed audit and recovers otherwise; own history & \const{7} & both tests; second test: headline only, and the six fresh markets in an exploratory follow-up after the report \\
   118	D2+H3 & D2 with a searched option to replace the identity after a failed audit & \const{8} & both tests; second test: all seven markets \\
   119	H2 & Sealed family written in a separate session; contains D2, no exit or re-entry & sealed & first test \\
   120	X7-SC & State-conditioned: fresh, established, and terminal phases from its own audit exposure and pass count; reset or wait out a ban; voluntary exit & \const{15} & second test, all seven markets \\
   121	X5-AG/v2 & Age-gated shading with the public constants & \const{7} & second test, all seven markets \\
   122	H4 & Sealed, defence-aware, written after every defender was frozen; loaded once after the draw & at most \const{15} & second test, headline \\
   123	W0, W1 & Welfare attacks on the D2+H3 and X7-SC knob spaces & as those & second test, headline \\
   124	X6-SE/v2 & Coalition of two or five with shared knobs and member schedules; members share their histories & \const{8} & second test, headline \\
   125	X8-DB & Coalition of two: a decoy that replaces its identity on a schedule and a beneficiary & \const{9} & second test, headline \\\bottomrule
   126	\end{tabular}
   127	\caption{Attacker families. Knob counts as registered; the supplement gives every bound.}\label{tab:families}
   128	\end{table}
   129	
   130	H4's code was committed only by its hash before the lock and released after the report review; it is in \texttt{sealed2/h4/} of the release (bundle SHA-256 \texttt{cba512aa\ldots}), with its author's note on which families it contains as special cases.
   131	
   132	\paragraph{Search quality.} Scheduled cells get a doubling check: the registered search continues with as many restarts again, and the cell is flagged if the doubled search improves the attacker's normalised profit or the reference's normalised profit (profit cells), the welfare attack's efficiency (welfare cells), or the coalition objective by at least \const{0.005}, or if a W1 search finds a feasible candidate where the original found none. A doubled search's winner is reported beside the cell and never replaces its frozen estimate. Cells without a scheduled check carry no search-quality claim. A cell is \emph{missing} if it did not complete or is invalid: not run (a cap stop or a cut), a refused sample, a nonfinite statistic, a search with no finite candidate, or a failed load of H4; a missing cell removes every decision that uses it. A \emph{search-flagged} cell keeps its decision, labelled as a decision about the prescribed search procedure, and leaves that rule's robustness statement for that family unanswered. The search bounds exposure to the searched families and optimiser; it does not certify robustness.
   133	
   134	\section{The first test (October 2026)}\label{sec:eval1}
   135	\paragraph{Claims.} The original claim was locked and timestamped on 17 October 2026, before the rule was implemented. At $\beta = \evone{0.05}$, the median position, and on 40 fresh seeds, its \emph{rule clause} required, for each confirmatory family, a one-sided 95\% lower bound of R3's gross efficiency of at least \evone{0.90}, a one-sided 95\% upper bound of its gain of at most \evone{0.30}, and audit cost within budget on every seed. Its \emph{baseline clause} required, for each of B2 and B3, a confirmatory family in which either the paired inflation-caused efficiency-loss difference exceeded \evone{0.05} or the baseline's gain exceeded \evone{1.0}, at the 98.75\% level. Amendment 1, registered after the baselines' development results showed that the baseline clause was unlikely to pass, added an amended claim on the public identity-replacement family D2+H3: the same absolute part as the rule clause (gross-efficiency lower bound at least \evone{0.90}, gain upper bound at most \evone{0.30}, budget compliance) and paired gain reductions of at least \evone{0.10} against B2 and B3 at the 98.75\% level, plus the H2 rule clause. After a development test killed the first rule, Amendment 3 registered one successor, R3, before its implementation.
   136	
   137	\paragraph{Sealing.} The sealed family H2 was written in a separate session from a pinned copy of the repository, and only its hash was committed. Before unsealing, at the author's request, the custodian disclosed that H2 contains D2; that H2 does not exit or re-enter was stated in the public specification before the lock.
