docs/revision/paper-v2/main.tex:40:\paragraph{Results in brief.} Q1: pooled over the six fresh markets, R3 and C$^*$ are ``\site{int_q1_cat}'' within the registered margin under X7-SC, and likewise under D2+H3 (the two families are close to one test, since X7-SC mostly selected the embedded D2+H3 winner): R3 is not materially better than the development-selected verified-history comparator at the same budget, and with the frozen searches the difference varies in sign across markets, while under D2+H3 with the doubled searches, and with each rule's strongest family, it is positive on every market. Q2: in the pooled factorial contrasts, the priority phase and newest-first targeting each lower the measured gain (main effects \site{int_q2_p} and \site{int_q2_g}), and either one alone accounts for most of the reduction. Q3: the training-selected attack envelopes have lower measured gains against R3 than against B3 on every evaluated market, a finite-search comparison that does not establish robustness to the searched families; the selected unconstrained welfare attack lowers R3's gross efficiency to \site{int_q3_w0}, and exploratory lotteries over existing attacks, selected on the evaluation records, reach efficiency well below the \site{int_w1} of the registered W1 search at non-negative estimated gain, with public-family attacks alone; the W1 search therefore supplies no efficiency bound for randomised policies, and the mixtures' population profitability is not established. Q4: the B3--R3 comparison passes on \site{int_q4_k} of six fresh markets, all three passes qualified by search flags; on the other three, R3's estimated advantage is positive but below the registered threshold.
docs/revision/paper-v2/main.tex:112:Every unilateral attacker family reads only the provider's own history across its identities, the round, its own parameters, and the rule's public constants $T$ and $L$; it does not observe the remaining global budget or other identities' ranks. Coalition members may share their members' own histories. For each parametrised family a registered search (CMA-ES with restarts on training seeds) finds the attack. A rule's truthful reference is then selected on training seeds from one menu built from its D2+H3 and X7-SC searches and frozen; the W1 welfare searches, which need that reference, run after the freeze; coalitions use their own staged joint truthful reference; and no evaluation-seed episode of a market runs before all of its choices are frozen.
docs/revision/paper-v2/main.tex:120:X7-SC & State-conditioned: fresh, established, and terminal phases from its own audit exposure and pass count; reset or wait out a ban; voluntary exit & \const{15} & second test, all seven markets \\
docs/revision/paper-v2/main.tex:123:W0, W1 & Welfare attacks on the D2+H3 and X7-SC knob spaces & as those & second test, headline \\
docs/revision/paper-v2/main.tex:163:\paragraph{Questions.} Q1: does R3 ($F_{111}$) leave a lower or higher identity-replacement gain than C$^*$, at the fixed pair? Q2: what do the priority phase, the residual targeting, and the pacing contribute, alone and together? Q3: do the results hold against X7-SC, X5-AG/v2, the defence-aware sealed family H4, welfare attacks, and coalitions, and does R4 repair the reserve collapse? Q4: does the first test's comparison of B3 and R3 under D2+H3 replicate on fresh markets? Q1 and Q4 are confirmatory; Q2 and Q3 are descriptive.
docs/revision/paper-v2/main.tex:176:Q1 & $d = g_{C^*} - g_{F_{111}}$, pooled, for D2+H3 and X7-SC. Direction: Holm's procedure~\cite{holm1979simple} on calibrated two-sided $p$-values for $d = \const{0}$. Size: the calibrated Bonferroni interval $I$ for $d$. Categories, in order: \emph{materially lower} if $I$ lies above $\const{0.05}$ (R3 lower) or below $\const{-0.05}$ (C$^*$ lower); \emph{equivalent} if $I$ lies within $[\const{-0.05}, \const{0.05}]$, with a direction label when Holm rejects $d = \const{0}$; \emph{ordered, size not established} if Holm rejects and neither applies; otherwise \emph{inconclusive}. A rule is called better only with the first category in its favour and a paired gross-efficiency difference (that rule minus the other) whose nominal one-sided \const{97.5}\% lower bound is at least $\const{-0.01}$ (descriptive; no calibrated joint guarantee). If that lower bound is below $\const{-0.01}$, the gain category stands and the result is labelled ``efficiency non-inferiority unresolved''; it is labelled ``lower gain with an efficiency loss'' only if the corresponding upper bound is below $\const{-0.01}$. \\
docs/revision/paper-v2/main.tex:191:\paragraph{Age-ranked versus history-based checking (Q1).} Pooled over the six fresh markets, the prescribed searches leave the identity-replacement attacker a measured gain against C$^*$ higher than against R3 by \site{r_q1_d_est} under D2+H3 and by \site{r_q1_x_est} under X7-SC; the mean gains are \site{r_abs_cs} against C$^*$ and \site{r_abs_r3} against R3 under D2+H3, and \site{r_abs_cs_x} and \site{r_abs_r3_x} under X7-SC. Both direction tests reject $d = \const{0}$ (calibrated input $p' = $ \site{r_q1_d_p}; Holm-adjusted $p = $ \site{r_q1_holm} for each). Both intervals lie inside the registered margin of $\pm\const{0.05}$: the categories are ``\site{r_q1_d_cat}'' (D2+H3) and ``\site{r_q1_x_cat}'' (X7-SC), and the separate efficiency label is ``\site{r_q1_d_eff}''. Under the registered margin, R3 is not materially better than the development-selected comparator; the margin of $\pm\const{0.05}$ is half the first test's comparative margin, not an estimated threshold of buyer indifference, and a narrower margin could order the two rules. The difference changes sign across markets: per market (descriptive), it ranges from \site{r_q1_f3_d} on fresh3 to \site{r_q1_f1_d} on fresh1 under D2+H3. Search uncertainty matters at this level: evaluating the doubled-search winners on the same seeds against the same frozen references (Table~\ref{tab:ph-doubled}, exploratory) changes fresh3's difference from \site{r_dbl_f3} to \site{r_dbl_f3_d}, and the pooled differences to \site{r_dbl_pool_d} under D2+H3 and \site{r_dbl_pool_x} under X7-SC; no decision is recomputed. The registered intervals measure seed uncertainty in six fixed markets. Treating the six markets as a sample of the neighbourhood (exploratory), a $t$ interval over the per-market differences is [\site{r_ml_lo}, \site{r_ml_hi}] under D2+H3, with \site{r_ml_pos} of six markets positive, and [\site{r_ml_xlo}, \site{r_ml_xhi}] under X7-SC; with the doubled-search winners it is [\site{r_ml_dlo}, \site{r_ml_dhi}] under D2+H3, with \site{r_ml_dpos} positive. With each rule's strongest training-selected family the interval is [\site{r_env_lo}, \site{r_env_hi}], with \site{r_env_pos} of six markets positive. The direction is therefore fairly stable across these analyses, while the size is not: every upper end is near the margin, so the equivalence label is the most fragile part; beyond these six markets the sign is not established either, since the frozen interval contains zero and the searches share inputs; on these markets the registered direction tests reject zero for the prescribed searches, and another comparator would be a different comparison. The frozen D2+H3 interval contains zero and crosses the margin, so this analysis does not establish the category for the neighbourhood; the X7-SC interval excludes zero and lies inside the margin, but that family mostly repeats D2+H3 (below). In relative terms, under D2+H3, the attacker's pooled mean gain against C$^*$ exceeds that against R3 by a fraction of \site{r_rel}. The two Q1 families are close to one test: X7-SC's search starts from the embedded D2+H3 winner, and in \site{r_x7_same} of the \site{r_x7_cells} Q1 cells it selected that winner, with identical evaluated gains; only fresh3 against C$^*$ and fresh4 against R3 differ. With the headline market, the same holds in \site{r_x7_all} of \site{r_x7_all_n} market--rule cells, which is why most of prediction Q3-a's differences are exactly zero. With each rule's strongest training-selected family instead (Table~\ref{tab:q3-envelope}), the pooled difference is \site{r_env_pool}, and \site{r_env_f1} on fresh1, where X5-AG/v2 is C$^*$'s strongest family. C$^*$ was selected in development on gross efficiency in effect, and the full-budget runner-up left a lower development gain than C$^*$ (Section~\ref{sec:rules}); a comparator selected on attacker gain could change the direction label. The two rules differ more in whom they burden than in the attacker's gain. In the registered descriptive honest-replacement diagnostic at the headline market (Table~\ref{tab:ph-honest}), an honest provider replaces its identity at round \const{50}: R3 audits \site{r_hon_r3_0} and \site{r_hon_r3_1} of the replacement's served jobs in its first two ten-round age bins, C$^*$ audits \site{r_hon_cs_0} and \site{r_hon_cs_1} (its lowest bin; \site{r_hon_cs_2} in the next), and B3 about \site{r_hon_b3_0}. Under R3 the replacement earns less per round (relative to C$^*$, a fraction of \site{r_hon_rel}; the registered diagnostic stores bin means, so no interval is available) and loses more receipts to refunds paid to buyers on false audit failures; R3 directs more of the fixed audit capacity to this provider, which does not show higher total audit spending. Both decisions are about the prescribed search procedure: the D2+H3 test uses search-flagged cells, and the X7-SC test uses them through its references (addendum); their nominal error guarantees are not established on the fresh, selected-rule distributions (Section~\ref{sec:design}). The paired descriptive differences in Table~\ref{tab:q1-side} are small for efficiency, audit spending, refunds on false audit failures, and the market service fraction; the reference-profit column is in raw profit units. Splitting $d$ with the same normalisation into the attacked-profit term and the reference-profit term (Table~\ref{tab:ph-q1}, exploratory) shows that almost all of it is attacked profit: the attacker earns more under C$^*$ when it deviates, not when it is truthful.
docs/revision/paper-v2/main.tex:246:\paragraph{Which parts of R3 matter (Q2).} Under separately searched D2+H3 attacks, pooled over the fresh markets, the main effects on the gain are \site{r_q2_p} for the priority phase P and \site{r_q2_g} for newest-first residual targeting G, both reductions, and \site{r_q2_s} for pacing S, a small increase (X7-SC: \site{r_q2_s_x}). The two targeting parts are substitutes: the PG factorial contrast, half the difference between G's conditional effects, is \site{r_q2_pg}; G changes the gain by \site{r_q2_g_p0} without the priority phase and by only \site{r_q2_g_p1} with it, and the priority phase changes it by only \site{r_q2_p_g1} once G is in place (averaged over both pacing settings). R3's own comparison, $F_{111}$ minus $F_{011}$ (exploratory, from the evaluation records), is \site{r_dp} pooled: exactly zero on four markets, where both rules leave the attacker the same gain, and nonzero on fresh1 (\site{r_dp_f1}) and fresh3. Against the fixed attacks, the priority phase's effect given newest-first targeting is larger (\site{r_q2_fixed_pg1} under D2+H3 and \site{r_q2_fixed_pg1_x} under X7-SC, averaged over pacing), though still small next to newest-first targeting's effect without the priority phase (above). In the pooled, separately searched comparisons, given newest-first targeting the priority phase adds little at these audit ratios and capacities; at a lower audit ratio or a larger attacker capacity, half of a round's audits may no longer cover one provider's jobs, and the substitution need not hold. In the pooled contrasts, either P or G alone accounts for most of the measured reduction; adding the other contributes little. The pattern also appears in the fixed-attack contrasts averaged over the eight selected attacks (P: \site{r_q2_fixed_p}). Our registered conjectures (P negligible, the targeting parts additive, S helpful) failed as scored (Table~\ref{tab:predictions}); the stricter eligibility reading of Table~\ref{tab:pred-eligibility} leaves them not assessed, with the estimates unchanged. X7-SC agrees (Table~\ref{tab:q2-x7sc}).
docs/revision/paper-v2/main.tex:255:\caption{Q2 under X7-SC, as in Table~\ref{tab:q2}.}\label{tab:q2-x7sc}
docs/revision/paper-v2/main.tex:263:\paragraph{Stronger attacks and where R3 fails (Q3).} Table~\ref{tab:q3-envelope} gives each core rule's attack envelope: the family with the highest training gain, evaluated unchanged. These are finite-search results, not bounds on the families. Against B3 the selected envelope reaches \site{r_q3_f000_f1} on fresh1; against R3 it is \site{r_q3_f111_f1} there, and against C$^*$ \site{r_q3_cs_f1}, from \site{r_q3_cs_f1_fam}. H4 was written in a separate session against the frozen defenders and committed by its hash before the lock; its author's containment note states that it contains D2+H3 exactly as a special case, so it extends the public family rather than offering a disjoint attack concept. Evaluated at the headline market only, its selected search gains \site{r_q3_f111_h4} against R3 and \site{r_q3_f000_h4} against B3. Robustness against D2+H3 is unanswered for every core rule because of search flags, and so is R3's robustness against X7-SC; X5-AG/v2 and H4 carry no search-quality claim. Welfare attacks are the clearest weakness. At the headline market, the selected W0 attacks (no profit constraint) yield gross efficiency \site{r_q3_w0_f000} for B3 and \site{r_q3_w0} for R3, with gains relative to the truthful reference of \site{r_q3_w0g_f000} and \site{r_q3_w0g_f111}; C$^*$ is like R3. Under the prescribed W1 searches (mean gain at least zero), the selected feasible attacks yield efficiency \site{r_q3_w1} for R3, while B3 stays at \site{r_q3_w1_f000} (Table~\ref{tab:q3-welfare}). R3's selected W1 policy has an estimated gain of exactly zero, and the doubled W1 searches for R3 and C$^*$ select truthful delivery (zero shading), with the option to replace after a failed audit left on: the W1 search found no profitable welfare attack against them. These finite-search results are not lower bounds on efficiency over a broader policy class. An episode-level mixture, which picks a welfare attack or a profitable attack at the start of each episode, has the probability-weighted mean of its components' gains and efficiencies, so it satisfies a mean-gain constraint only when those weighted gains do; the following rows are that arithmetic on the existing evaluation records. With the H4 attack as companion, at W0 weights selected on the evaluation records, the mixtures reach gross efficiencies of \site{r_ph_e_r3} for R3 and \site{r_ph_e_cs} for C$^*$ at non-negative estimated gain; with public D2+H3 companions, R3's mixture (\site{r_ph_e_r3_d}) meets the registered training constraint, and C$^*$'s does so at the smaller weight \site{r_ph_fw_cs}, with efficiency \site{r_ph_fe_cs} against \site{r_ph_w1_cs} under the registered W1 search (Tables~\ref{tab:ph-welfare} and~\ref{tab:ph-welfare-sens}). Weights and companions were chosen on the evaluation seeds with an unadjusted nominal one-sided \const{95} percent bound, so these rows show that the prescribed W1 search did not consider episode-level mixtures that reach lower empirical efficiency; they are not out-of-sample estimates, and population profitability remains unestablished. The mixture efficiencies stay above \const{0.90}; the analysis does not touch Q4 or its registered predicate. For coalitions of two synchronised members, the selected total gains are \site{r_q3_coal_f000} against B3 and \site{r_q3_coal_f111_c2} against R3 (search flagged); for five, \site{r_q3_coal_f000_c5} and \site{r_q3_coal_f111} (total objective), while the policy selected for the minimum-member objective leaves R3's worst member a mean gain of \site{r_q3_coal_f111_c5min}; the decoy-and-beneficiary family gives \site{r_q3_db_f000} against B3 and \site{r_q3_db_f111} against R3. These comparisons establish neither coalition stability nor an ordering across every coalition family (Table~\ref{tab:q3-coalitions}). At $\rho = \const{1}$, where R3's terminal reserve collapses, R3 leaves \site{r_rep_f111} under D2+H3; the repair R4 lowers this to \site{r_rep_r4} and C$^*$ with the capped schedule (C$^*$/S2) to \site{r_rep_cs2} (Table~\ref{tab:q3-repair}).
docs/revision/paper-v2/main.tex:297:\paragraph{Search quality.} No cell is missing. \site{r_flag} of the \site{r_flag_n} doubling checks are flagged, and fresh-market factorial cells outside Q1 and Q4 have no doubling check, so their narrow intervals describe seed noise only. A nesting comparison on the existing records (exploratory) shows evaluation discrepancies that call for further search checks, also in unflagged cells: X7-SC contains D2+H3, yet in \site{r_nest_over} of \site{r_nest_n} cells the X7-SC winner's evaluated gain falls short of the better of the frozen and doubled D2+H3 winners on the same seeds by more than the doubling threshold (at most \site{r_nest_max}). Winners are selected on training seeds and need not keep their order on evaluation seeds, and picking the better D2+H3 winner on the evaluation seeds favours it, so this is a discrepancy, not a demonstrated search failure. The report lists the search-flagged cells, and the addendum traces the inherited flags of every Q1 and Q4 decision through the records' references. Q2 and Q3 statements are descriptive; the cells they use carry the flags listed in the report. Table~\ref{tab:ph-stability} sets the Q1 estimates beside their re-evaluation with the registered configurations, and Table~\ref{tab:ph-q4-stability} compares the Q4 estimates, calibrated lower bounds, and decisions under both configurations and gives the adjusted market $p$-value under the registered configuration; the largest Q4 change is on fresh4, where the gain difference moves from \site{r_st_f4_old} to \site{r_st_f4_new}, still above the registered \const{0.10}.
docs/revision/lessons.md:89:- **Mistake 1:** I adopted Fable r3's "nesting failure" reading of a negative held-out I, and its rule that the doubled value is "more reliable", although the paper's own X7-SC caveat says held-out order need not follow nesting. Sol and Astra did not catch it either.
"""Tests of the reset block (docs/revision/reset-experiment-plan.md, draft 5, sections 5 and 6), written before the code."""
import json
import sys
from pathlib import Path

import numpy as np
import pytest

sys.path.insert(0, str(Path(__file__).resolve().parent))
import reset_block as RB  # noqa: E402

M6 = tuple(f"fresh{i}" for i in range(6))


def _cells(i_b3=0.3, i_r3=0.01, i_c=0.05, g_r3_d2h3=0.05, g_r3_d2=0.03, n=40, noise=0.0, seed=0):
    """Synthetic per-seed gains: D2+H3 gains are D2 gains plus I; B3-R3 gap under D2 is 0.01 by construction."""
    rng = np.random.default_rng(seed); cells = {}
    for m in M6:
        e = rng.normal(0, noise, n) if noise else np.zeros(n)
        d2 = {"F111": g_r3_d2 + e, "F000": g_r3_d2 + 0.01 + e, "C*": g_r3_d2 + 0.005 + e}
        d2h3 = {"F111": d2["F111"] + i_r3, "F000": d2["F000"] + i_b3, "C*": d2["C*"] + i_c}
        cells[m] = {"D2+H3": d2h3, "D2": d2}
    return cells


def test_point_estimates_of_i_contrasts_gaps_and_share():
    s = RB.statistics(_cells())
    assert s["I"]["F000"]["pooled"] == pytest.approx(0.3) and s["I"]["F111"]["pooled"] == pytest.approx(0.01)
    assert s["contrast"]["B3-R3"]["pooled"] == pytest.approx(0.29) and s["contrast"]["C*-R3"]["pooled"] == pytest.approx(0.04)
    assert s["G"]["D2"]["pooled"] == pytest.approx(0.01) and s["G"]["D2+H3"]["pooled"] == pytest.approx(0.30)
    assert s["S"]["pooled"] == pytest.approx(1 - 0.01 / 0.30)


def test_pooled_is_the_equal_weight_mean_of_market_means():
    c = _cells(); c["fresh0"]["D2+H3"]["F000"] = c["fresh0"]["D2+H3"]["F000"] + 0.6   # I(B3) = 0.9 at fresh0 only
    s = RB.statistics(c)
    assert s["I"]["F000"]["markets"]["fresh0"] == pytest.approx(0.9)
    assert s["I"]["F000"]["pooled"] == pytest.approx((0.9 + 5 * 0.3) / 6)


def test_share_undefined_when_the_denominator_lower_bound_is_at_or_below_005():
    c = _cells(i_b3=0.04, i_r3=0.01, noise=0.02)          # pooled G(D2+H3) = 0.04: lower bound below 0.05
    r = RB.analyse(c, b=999, seed=1)
    assert r["S"]["pooled"]["value"] is None and r["S"]["pooled"]["reason"].startswith("not defined")
    assert r["G"]["D2+H3"]["pooled"]["estimate"] == pytest.approx(0.04)  # the gaps are reported anyway


def test_share_defined_and_not_clipped():
    c = _cells(i_b3=0.3, i_r3=0.01)
    for m in M6:                                           # G(D2) negative, so S > 1
        c[m]["D2"]["F000"] = c[m]["D2"]["F111"] - 0.05; c[m]["D2+H3"]["F000"] = c[m]["D2"]["F000"] + 0.3
    r = RB.analyse(c, b=199, seed=1)
    assert r["S"]["pooled"]["value"] > 1


def test_bootstrap_uses_the_same_seed_indices_for_every_rule_and_family_of_a_market():
    c = _cells(noise=0.05, seed=3)                         # identical noise in every rule and family of a market
    r = RB.analyse(c, b=499, seed=2)
    lo, hi = r["G"]["D2"]["pooled"]["ci95"]               # the shared noise cancels in a paired contrast
    assert hi - lo == pytest.approx(0.0, abs=1e-12)
    lo, hi = r["I"]["F000"]["pooled"]["ci95"]
    assert hi - lo == pytest.approx(0.0, abs=1e-12)


def test_bootstrap_is_reproducible_and_has_9999_resamples_by_default():
    assert RB.B == 9999
    c = _cells(noise=0.05, seed=4)
    for m in M6:                                           # break the pairing so intervals have width
        c[m]["D2"]["F111"] = c[m]["D2"]["F111"] + np.random.default_rng(9).normal(0, 0.05, 40)
    a, b = RB.analyse(c, b=299, seed=5), RB.analyse(c, b=299, seed=5)
    assert a["G"]["D2"]["pooled"]["ci95"] == b["G"]["D2"]["pooled"]["ci95"]
    lo, hi = a["G"]["D2"]["pooled"]["ci95"]
    assert lo < a["G"]["D2"]["pooled"]["estimate"] < hi


def test_market_sensitivity_sign_counts_and_leave_one_out_share():
    c = _cells(); c["fresh2"]["D2+H3"]["F000"] = c["fresh2"]["D2"]["F000"] - 0.02     # I(B3) < I(R3) at fresh2
    r = RB.analyse(c, b=199, seed=1)
    assert r["sensitivity"]["signs"]["B3-R3"] == {"positive": 5, "n": 6}
    assert r["sensitivity"]["signs"]["C*-R3"] == {"positive": 6, "n": 6}
    loo = r["sensitivity"]["S_leave_one_out"]
    assert set(loo) == set(M6) and all(v["value"] is not None for v in loo.values())


def test_leave_one_out_has_its_own_five_market_gate():
    c = _cells(i_b3=0.3)
    for m in M6[1:]:                                       # only fresh0 carries a B3 - R3 gap under D2+H3
        c[m]["D2+H3"]["F000"] = c[m]["D2+H3"]["F111"] + 0.0
    r = RB.analyse(c, b=199, seed=1)
    assert r["sensitivity"]["S_leave_one_out"]["fresh0"]["value"] is None     # five markets left: no gap
"""External review r3 (Fable 5.2), problem 2: do the 18 frozen truthful references replace their identity on the
evaluation seeds, and how much is that worth to them? Exploratory, run on the Mac.

`run`: for each (market, rule), the chosen reference candidate, its family and knobs, reset counts, the reference's mean
normalised profit, and (if it resets) the same policy's mean normalised profit with replacement disabled (X7-SC: h_R = T
and lambda = 1; D2+H3: x = 0). It records, without requiring equality, how far the Mac run is from the stored Linux
reference profits (`validation_*`): the Mac and Linux differ on some seeds (lesson in commit 9c13b88).

`summarize`: combines the per-market cell files with reset.json. A reference without replacement earns less by its
replacement value, so every gain against it rises by that value; G = g(B3) - g(R3) under either family shifts by
value(B3) - value(R3), the same for D2+H3 and D2. This is a sensitivity calculation on the stored gains, not a
re-evaluation.

Usage:
  ref_resets.py run CODE_ROOT BUNDLE POD_RECORDS OUT.json [MARKET]
  ref_resets.py summarize RESET_JSON OUT.json CELLS.json [CELLS.json ...]"""
import json
import sys
from pathlib import Path

import numpy as np

MARKETS = tuple(f"fresh{i}@rho0.2" for i in range(6))


def no_replace(family, knobs, T):
    if family == "X7-SC":
        return {**knobs, "h_R": float(T), "lambda": 1.0}
    if "x" in knobs:
        return {**knobs, "x": 0}
    return None


def summarize(cells: dict, reset: dict) -> dict:
    rv = lambda k: cells[k].get("replacement_value_to_reference") or 0.0   # noqa: E731 (no reset: no value)
    dG = {m.split("@")[0]: rv(f"{m}|F000") - rv(f"{m}|F111") for m in MARKETS}
    dC = {m.split("@")[0]: rv(f"{m}|C*") - rv(f"{m}|F111") for m in MARKETS}
    g1 = reset["base"]["G"]["D2+H3"]["pooled"]["estimate"]; g0 = reset["base"]["G"]["D2"]["pooled"]["estimate"]
    dp = sum(dG.values()) / len(MARKETS)
    return {"n": len(cells), "refs_with_any_reset": sum(1 for v in cells.values() if v.get("mean_resets", 0) > 0),
            "max_abs_replacement_value": max(abs(rv(k)) for k in cells),
            "G_shift_per_market": dG, "G_shift_pooled": dp, "Cstar_minus_R3_shift_pooled": sum(dC.values()) / len(MARKETS),
            "S_with_non_replacing_references": 1 - (g0 + dp) / (g1 + dp),
            "max_abs_validation_mean_diff": max(abs(v["validation_mean_diff"]) for v in cells.values())}


def run(T0: Path, B: Path, POD: Path, OUT: Path, only=None) -> None:
    sys.path.insert(0, str(T0 / "docs/revision/followup"))
    import reset_followup_v2 as F
    R = F.R
    from sim.cell import SeedCache, run_focal
    from sim.records import cfg_from_dict
    from sim.records_v2 import read
    root = json.loads((B / "draw/draw.json").read_text())["hash"]
    salt = json.loads((B / "draw/instances.json").read_text())["salt_sha256"]
    cfgs = {k: cfg_from_dict(v) for k, v in json.loads((B / "deviation/cfg_linux.json").read_text()).items()}
    jobs = F.build_jobs(root, cfgs, json.loads((T0 / "experiments/v2/params.json").read_text()), salt)
    res = {}
    for j in jobs:
        ctx, rule = j["context"], j["rule"]
        if only and not ctx.startswith(only + "@"):
            continue
        ref = read(B / "eval-all" / "reference" / ctx / (rule.replace("*", "star") + ".json"))
        cand = ref["candidates"][ref["choice"]]
        T, L = R.constants(j); cell = R.eval_cell(j); cache = SeedCache()
        row = {"name": cand["name"], "family": cand.get("family"), "knobs": cand.get("knobs")}
        if not cand.get("family"):
            row["note"] = "no family (abstain or honest policy)"; res[f"{ctx}|{rule}"] = row; continue
        fam = R.family_for(cand["family"], T, L)
        out = run_focal(cell, lambda: fam.make(dict(cand["knobs"])), cache)
        st = F.reset_stats(out.results, cell.focal); norm = np.asarray(out.normalized, float)
        ev = json.loads((POD / "evaluate" / (j["id"].replace("|", "__").replace("*", "star") + ".json")).read_text())
        pr = np.asarray(ev["profit_ref"], float) / (np.asarray(ev["sstar"], float) / cell.cfg.N)
        row.update({k: st[k] for k in ("mean_resets", "p_any_reset", "mean_ban_rounds")})
        row.update({"ref_mean_normalized": float(norm.mean()), "validation_mean_diff": float(np.mean(norm - pr)),
                    "validation_seeds_differing": int((np.abs(norm - pr) > 1e-9).sum()), "validation_max_abs_diff": float(np.max(np.abs(norm - pr)))})
        if st["mean_resets"] > 0:
            k0 = no_replace(cand["family"], cand["knobs"], T)
            off = run_focal(cell, lambda: fam.make(dict(k0)), cache)
            row["ref_no_replace_mean_normalized"] = float(np.mean(off.normalized))
            row["replacement_value_to_reference"] = float(norm.mean() - np.mean(off.normalized))
        res[f"{ctx}|{rule}"] = row
        print(ctx, rule, row["name"], row["family"], "resets", row["mean_resets"], "repl_value", row.get("replacement_value_to_reference"), flush=True)
    OUT.write_text(json.dumps(res, indent=1) + "\n")


LABEL = ("external review r3 problem 2 (exploratory, Mac): the 18 frozen truthful references on the evaluation seeds; "
         "replacement_value_to_reference = reference normalised profit minus the same policy with replacement disabled "
         "(X7-SC: h_R = T, lambda = 1; D2+H3: x = 0). The summary adds these Mac values to the stored Linux gains (a "
         "sensitivity calculation, not a re-evaluation). The Mac reproduces the stored reference profits on average "
         "(validation_mean_diff) but not seed for seed (Mac vs Linux; commit 9c13b88).")


def main(argv):
    if argv[0] == "run":
        run(Path(argv[1]), Path(argv[2]), Path(argv[3]), Path(argv[4]), argv[5] if len(argv) > 5 else None)
    elif argv[0] == "summarize":
        cells = {}
        for f in argv[3:]:
            cells.update(json.loads(Path(f).read_text()))

