 Referee report: Checking Newcomers When
 Identities Are Free

 1. Files opened and review scope
 I opened all the supplied material relevant to this review; no supplied file failed to open. Reading, parsing
 records, and reproducing a simulation are different levels of verification:

  • Paper: main.pdf, all 33 pages, read in consecutive ranges 1–6, 7–12, 13–18, 19–25, and 26–33. I also
    inspected rendered pages containing the reset discussion and Table 11.

  • Reviews and response: PREVIOUS_REVIEW.md, PREVIOUS_REVIEW_R2.md, and RESPONSE.md, read after
    forming an initial assessment of the paper.

  • Reset implementation: reset-experiment-plan.md; reset_block.py; test_reset_block.py;
    reset_followup_v2.py; reset.json; posthoc_reset.tex; reset_reference/ref_resets.py;
    reset_reference/test_ref_summary.py; all six cells_fresh*.json files; and
    reset_reference/reset_reference.json.

  • Reset and registered records: every supplied JSON under records/eval_all/evaluate/, records/reset/, and
    records/reference/; records/report.json; records/diagnostics.json; and records/spot_compare.json. Large
    record collections were inspected programmatically, including the arrays used in the calculations below.

  • Registration and other analyses: protocol-v2.yaml; predictions-v2.md; revision-program-design.md,
    especially sections 6–9; addendum.md; stability-result.json; audit.json and audit_v2.py; eligibility.json;
    scoring.json, score_v2.py, and test_scoring.py; posthoc.json and posthoc_v2.py; every row of
    lottery_seeds.csv and lottery_train.csv; h4-family.py and h4-containment.md; and operations-ledger.md. For
    report.md, I inspected its structure, prose, and relevant numerical summaries; I did not manually read every
    element of its large inline arrays.

 Execution: the requested reset command and reference-summary command completed. Reset output agrees
 exactly except for input-path strings; the generated Table 11 source and reference summary are byte-identical
 to the supplied files. Independent calculations also checked gain normalization, principal registered contrasts,
 p-value bookkeeping, lottery arithmetic, and eligibility tallies.

 Not independently executable here: the simulator and frozen runner are absent, as disclosed in the prompt.
 Search archives, freeze records, and some diagnostic inputs are also absent. Thus I could not rerun the
 simulation, authenticate the complete execution history, or inspect the original training-objective ordering. The
 complete scorer/audit suites require missing modules. The reset test files opened, but pytest is unavailable in
 this environment; I do not claim their tests passed. These are limits of this review package, not demonstrated
 failures of the paper.

 Sources below use the supplied filenames and exact JSON paths. R means the independently regenerated
 reset.json; RR means reset_reference/reset_reference.json. F and D mean frozen and doubled searches. Page
 references are to the supplied main.pdf.



 2. Summary verdict
 The reset result survives numerical scrutiny and adds a useful mechanism diagnosis: the large pooled B3–R3
 contrast under the replacement-capable search nearly vanishes under the no-replacement D2 search, with
 fixed-policy switch diagnostics supporting that interpretation. Most round-3 reporting requests are now
 addressed. However, the revision introduces or retains several avoidable inferential errors: held-out policy
 ordering is called a violation of family nesting; restoring the expected ordering is used to favor the doubled
 estimate; reference replacement is incorrectly described as always beneficial; and the abstract claims an
 unmeasured search-error bound. These require correction, not wholesale rejection of the calculations. My
 scores are WEIS 2027: 2/5, near borderline; AAMAS main track: 2/5; ACM EC: 1/5. The limiting factor is the



Independent referee review | 11 November 2026                                                                        1
Checking Newcomers When Identities Are Free | Review of r12 package



 contribution established by this particular environment and search procedure, especially the concentration of
 replacement activity in the focal provider. Disclosed failures and unsuccessful predictions are not themselves
 reasons for these scores.



