 succeeded in 0ms:
 by one market. However, “disappear” should mean a much smaller pooled estimate, not a proved zero
 population effect or disappearance in every market. For example, the doubled D2 C∗−R3 difference on fresh5
 is −0.047035, despite its pooled value of −0.003466; these are my paired-mean calculations from the
 corresponding held-out arrays in records/reset/doubling/.

 S above one is legitimate arithmetic because its numerator gap is negative. It is not a percentage of an
 identified causal effect that somehow exceeds the whole. Two different uncertainty statements coexist: the
 seed-bootstrap interval excludes zero for the average D2 gap in these fixed markets, while the exploratory



Independent referee review | 11 November 2026                                                                                           5
Checking Newcomers When Identities Are Free | Review of r12 package



 interval across market means includes zero. The latter does not establish equivalence. The added
 leave-one-out analysis usefully limits an interpretation of the small negative pooled gap as a stable reversal.

 The reference sensitivity uses the correct signed algebra. Define v_r as the normalized profit of the original
 frozen reference minus the same reference policy with replacement disabled. Then changing the benchmark
 changes gain by v_r. Both B3–R3 gaps shift by v_B3−v_R3, while I_r is unchanged. Thus the interaction
 I(B3)−I(R3) is reference-free, although G and S are not. The small computed pooled adjustment supports the
 reported sensitivity; it does not validate transferring a Mac counterfactual difference into Linux gains as an
 exact replay. Nor does disabling a switch reoptimize the truthful reference within a no-replacement policy
 class.

 I would retain the sensitivity with those limits. A complete resolution would replay both frozen-reference
 policies on the recorded Linux environment and, only if a different optimized-benchmark estimand is intended,
 select a separate non-replacing truthful reference under a newly fixed procedure. That additional computation
 is not required merely to report the current descriptive interaction honestly.


 (d) Contribution
 This follow-up strengthens the mechanism account, rather than changing any registered decision. The original
 comparison measured a rule difference with replacement available. The new comparison measures how that
 difference changes when the D2 attacker lacks replacement, while fixed-policy interventions show the switch
 matters substantially more to the selected B3 attacker than to the selected R3 attacker.

 The supported contribution is an interaction between audit design and the prescribed attacker family. It does
 not establish that age targeting uniquely causes the improvement, that R3 is optimal, or that the result
 survives competition from benign newcomers. A natural interpretation is that replacement-sensitive targeting
 restores some enforcement pressure that uniform auditing loses when bans can be evaded. C∗ is another
 such design.

 This distinction matters for novelty. Cheap-pseudonym newcomer costs and credibility-based checking with
 rejoining are established subjects, as the paper acknowledges. I verified primary sources for Friedman and
 Resnick’s The Social Cost of Cheap Pseudonyms and Sarmenta’s Sabotage-Tolerance Mechanisms for
 Volunteer Computing Systems. The contribution must therefore come from the controlled budgeted
 comparison, decomposition, and measured boundaries, not the general idea of scrutinizing newcomers. Stable
 sources: https://presnick.people.si.umich.edu/papers/identifiers/ and
 https://groups.csail.mit.edu/cag/bayanihan/papers/ccgrid01/ccgrid01.pdf.



 5. New or remaining problems, ranked by severity

 1. A held-out ordering reversal is misclassified, then used to favor an analysis
 Exact text: “With the frozen searches, two cells violate the nesting of D2 in D2+H3: I is −0.002 for C∗ on
 fresh3 and −0.006 for R3 on fresh4. The doubled searches remove both, so for the C∗ contrast the doubled
 value is the more reliable one.” (p. 16.)

 Nesting orders optimal values under a common objective. It does not order held-out outcomes of different
 training-selected policies. The paper itself gives the correct qualification for X7-SC on p. 19: “Winners are
 selected on training seeds and need not keep their order on evaluation seeds...” The two implicated D2+H3
 cells independently have doubling flags, so search sensitivity is real. That still does not make restoration of the
 expected held-out signs a criterion for choosing the more reliable estimate.

 Exact fix, text only: “In two frozen comparisons, the evaluated D2+H3 winner earns less than the evaluated D2
 winner: I is −0.002 for C∗ on fresh3 and −0.006 for R3 on fresh4. These reversals can reflect finite search or
 differences between training and evaluation outcomes. They disappear under doubling, which does not
 establish convergence. We retain frozen searches as primary and report the doubled results as sensitivity.”

 Analysis on existing records: with the original search archives supplied, compare recorded training-objective
 values of the embedded D2 candidate and selected D2+H3 candidate under a common objective. If that
 comparison requires fresh simulator calls, it needs compute. Rename nesting_failures to



Independent referee review | 11 November 2026                                                                          6
Checking Newcomers When Identities Are Free | Review of r12 package



 heldout_order_reversals, or explicitly document the existing field as a limited diagnostic. I reject the earlier
 referee’s stronger inference here.


 2. The abstract claims a search-error comparison that was never established
 Exact text: “Second, R3’s two targeting parts substitute for each other: either alone accounts for most of the
 pooled reduction in the attacker’s gain, and in R3’s own comparison (with and without the priority window,
 under D2+H3) the window changes the gain by −0.003, which is within search error.” (p. 1.)

 The number is a measured contrast. “Within search error” implies an error assessment for that contrast, but
 the fresh F011 factorial cells have no scheduled doubling check. Observed optimizer improvements elsewhere,
 or the flagging threshold, do not supply its uncertainty bound. The paper explicitly says the relevant narrow
 intervals measure seed noise only (p. 19).

 Exact fix, text only: “In R3’s own comparison, the priority window changes the pooled measured gain by only
 −0.003 under D2+H3, but search uncertainty for this contrast is unquantified.”

 Needs compute only if the stronger claim is retained: extend and replicate both sides of that specific search
 contrast under a declared procedure. Even then, observed sensitivity is not an optimality bound.


 3. “Disappears” and “not distinguishable” need explicit aggregation and uncertainty
 targets
 Exact text: the abstract’s disappearance sentence quoted in section 4(c), and “...but that gap is not
 distinguishable from zero...” (p. 15).

 The paper now provides the necessary evidence, but the phrasing allows readers to equate a small pooled
 estimate or a non-rejection with a zero effect. It also leaves the seed-level versus market-level distinction
 implicit at the critical sentence.

 Exact abstract fix, text only: “An exploratory D2-family follow-up, specified after the registered outcomes were
 seen, reduces the pooled B3 minus R3 gap from 0.173 to −0.009 and the pooled C∗ minus R3 gap from 0.021
 to −0.003 when attacker replacement is disabled, using the prescribed searches and unchanged references.”

 Exact p. 15 fix, text only: “The pooled D2 gap is slightly negative. Its paired-seed interval excludes zero
 conditional on these markets and selected policies, whereas the exploratory interval across market means
 includes zero. We therefore do not establish a negative average gap across the market neighbourhood.”

 The replacement numbers are the rounded regenerated values in section 4(b), not new estimates selected for
 this review.



